1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,627
It's a Tuesday morning.

2
00:00:01,527 --> 00:00:03,427
Your car makes a sound it has

3
00:00:03,327 --> 00:00:04,544
never made before.

4
00:00:04,624 --> 00:00:06,669
A loud, ugly grinding sound.

5
00:00:06,749 --> 00:00:09,147
Two hours later, the mechanic says it.

6
00:00:09,047 --> 00:00:10,356
Six hundred dollars.

7
00:00:10,436 --> 00:00:11,722
And you... just nod.

8
00:00:11,622 --> 00:00:13,027
You don't even flinch.

9
00:00:13,207 --> 00:00:14,816
Because three months ago,

10
00:00:14,716 --> 00:00:16,265
you started a tiny plan.

11
00:00:16,345 --> 00:00:18,872
And today, sitting in a separate account,

12
00:00:18,772 --> 00:00:21,063
there's exactly one thousand dollars.

13
00:00:21,143 --> 00:00:23,072
Your first thousand. Your shield.

14
00:00:23,152 --> 00:00:25,875
And that changes everything about this Tuesday.

15
00:00:26,055 --> 00:00:27,992
Now meet Jake. Same week.

16
00:00:27,892 --> 00:00:30,124
Same kind of car. Same sound.

17
00:00:30,204 --> 00:00:32,381
Jake actually earns more than you.

18
00:00:32,281 --> 00:00:34,763
Nine hundred dollars more, every month.

19
00:00:34,843 --> 00:00:37,294
But when the mechanic says six hundred dollars...

20
00:00:37,374 --> 00:00:38,834
Jake's stomach drops.

21
00:00:39,014 --> 00:00:40,464
He checks his bank app.

22
00:00:40,364 --> 00:00:42,285
Two hundred and twelve dollars.

23
00:00:42,365 --> 00:00:44,192
Rent is due in nine days.

24
00:00:44,272 --> 00:00:46,167
So he does what most people do.

25
00:00:46,067 --> 00:00:48,020
He hands over the credit card...

26
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:49,352
and hopes for the best.

27
00:00:49,532 --> 00:00:51,630
And here's the part nobody tells you.

28
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:53,591
At twenty-two percent interest,

29
00:00:53,491 --> 00:00:55,716
paying twenty-five dollars a month...

30
00:00:55,796 --> 00:00:59,148
that six-hundred-dollar repair takes Jake almost three

31
00:00:59,048 --> 00:01:00,172
years to pay off.

32
00:01:00,252 --> 00:01:02,695
And it costs him about eight hundred dollars.

33
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:04,631
Same repair. Same week.

34
00:01:04,711 --> 00:01:06,711
For you, it's an annoying Tuesday.

35
00:01:06,791 --> 00:01:09,773
For Jake, it's a two-hundred-dollar mistake that

36
00:01:09,673 --> 00:01:11,094
follows him for years.

37
00:01:11,174 --> 00:01:13,498
And the only difference between them...

38
00:01:13,398 --> 00:01:14,069
is a plan.

39
00:01:14,249 --> 00:01:15,913
And Jake is not alone.

40
00:01:15,813 --> 00:01:16,980
Not even close.

41
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:20,065
In Bankrate's latest emergency savings survey,

42
00:01:19,965 --> 00:01:22,528
fewer than half of American adults said

43
00:01:22,428 --> 00:01:26,507
they could cover a thousand-dollar surprise from their savings.

44
00:01:26,587 --> 00:01:27,779
Fewer than half!

45
00:01:27,959 --> 00:01:29,150
So stay with me.

46
00:01:29,230 --> 00:01:31,159
Because in the next few minutes,

47
00:01:31,059 --> 00:01:33,046
I'll show you exactly how you got

48
00:01:32,946 --> 00:01:35,275
to one thousand dollars in ninety days.

49
00:01:35,355 --> 00:01:36,531
Not with a raise.

50
00:01:36,431 --> 00:01:38,114
Not with a side business.

51
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:39,443
With six small moves.

52
00:01:39,523 --> 00:01:40,729
Stay till the end,

53
00:01:40,629 --> 00:01:42,881
because the math surprised even me.

54
00:01:43,061 --> 00:01:45,402
Let's rewind. Ninety days earlier.

55
00:01:45,482 --> 00:01:49,022
You earn thirty-two hundred dollars a month after taxes.

56
00:01:49,102 --> 00:01:50,886
Rent, car, phone, food...

57
00:01:50,786 --> 00:01:52,839
it all goes out. Every month.

58
00:01:52,919 --> 00:01:54,444
And at the end of the month,

59
00:01:54,344 --> 00:01:56,479
there's... nothing left. Sound familiar?

60
00:01:56,659 --> 00:01:59,410
You'd read about emergency funds before.

61
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:01,531
Three to six months of expenses.

62
00:02:01,611 --> 00:02:03,906
And honestly? That number made you want

63
00:02:03,806 --> 00:02:04,525
to give up.

64
00:02:04,605 --> 00:02:06,487
Ten thousand dollars? Fifteen?

65
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:07,496
On your paycheck?

66
00:02:07,576 --> 00:02:09,435
So you just... never started.

67
00:02:09,615 --> 00:02:12,288
So you made one decision that changed everything.

68
00:02:12,368 --> 00:02:15,305
Forget the mountain. Just get to one thousand.

69
00:02:15,385 --> 00:02:17,808
One thousand dollars is small enough to

70
00:02:17,708 --> 00:02:20,072
reach... and big enough to handle most

71
00:02:19,972 --> 00:02:22,217
of the surprises life throws at you.

72
00:02:22,297 --> 00:02:24,619
Then you'd worry about the big mountain later.

73
00:02:24,799 --> 00:02:26,633
A flat tire. A vet bill.

74
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:29,306
A dentist visit. A plane ticket home.

75
00:02:29,386 --> 00:02:31,909
Most of those fit under a thousand dollars.

76
00:02:31,989 --> 00:02:33,876
That's why it's the first goal.

77
00:02:33,776 --> 00:02:34,971
Not the last one...

78
00:02:34,871 --> 00:02:35,778
the first one.

79
00:02:35,858 --> 00:02:37,622
Small goal. Big relief.

80
00:02:37,802 --> 00:02:40,186
Move number one took you twelve minutes.

81
00:02:40,086 --> 00:02:41,328
On your lunch break.

82
00:02:41,408 --> 00:02:43,769
You opened a brand-new savings account.

83
00:02:43,849 --> 00:02:45,370
Not at your usual bank.

84
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:46,545
At a different one.

85
00:02:46,625 --> 00:02:48,423
One with no monthly fees.

86
00:02:48,603 --> 00:02:49,886
Why a different bank?

87
00:02:49,786 --> 00:02:51,350
Because you know yourself.

88
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:53,324
If the savings sit right next to

89
00:02:53,224 --> 00:02:55,847
your checking account, one tap moves it back.

90
00:02:55,927 --> 00:02:57,481
Late at night. On a sale.

91
00:02:57,381 --> 00:02:58,237
On a bad day.

92
00:02:58,417 --> 00:03:00,101
But if it's at another bank,

93
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:01,459
it takes a day or two to

94
00:03:01,359 --> 00:03:02,534
get the money back.

95
00:03:02,614 --> 00:03:03,733
And those two days?

96
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:05,451
They're your cooling-off period.

97
00:03:05,531 --> 00:03:08,148
Most impulse buys don't survive two days.

98
00:03:08,228 --> 00:03:10,325
Annoying? That's exactly the point.

99
00:03:10,505 --> 00:03:12,814
You picked a high-yield savings account,

100
00:03:12,714 --> 00:03:15,079
so the money earns a little interest too.

101
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:17,399
Rates change, so it's not a fortune.

102
00:03:17,479 --> 00:03:19,320
But it's a lot better than the

103
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:21,294
almost-zero your old account paid.

104
00:03:21,474 --> 00:03:24,312
And you gave the account a name. Not 'Savings'.

105
00:03:24,392 --> 00:03:26,701
You called it... 'Car Breaks Down Fund'.

106
00:03:26,781 --> 00:03:28,434
Sounds silly? It's not.

107
00:03:28,334 --> 00:03:30,257
A name with a purpose is so

108
00:03:30,157 --> 00:03:31,608
much harder to raid.

109
00:03:31,688 --> 00:03:33,562
Every time you open the app,

110
00:03:33,462 --> 00:03:34,766
it reminds you why.

111
00:03:34,946 --> 00:03:36,918
Move number two is the big one.

112
00:03:36,998 --> 00:03:39,248
You set up an automatic transfer.

113
00:03:39,148 --> 00:03:41,072
Fifty dollars. Every payday.

114
00:03:41,152 --> 00:03:43,174
You get paid every two weeks...

115
00:03:43,074 --> 00:03:45,469
so that's six paydays in ninety days.

116
00:03:45,649 --> 00:03:48,247
Here's the secret. The transfer happens the

117
00:03:48,147 --> 00:03:50,163
same morning your paycheck lands.

118
00:03:50,243 --> 00:03:52,054
Before rent. Before groceries.

119
00:03:51,954 --> 00:03:53,764
Before you even see the money.

120
00:03:53,844 --> 00:03:55,302
You pay yourself first.

121
00:03:55,382 --> 00:03:57,017
Whatever's left after that?

122
00:03:56,917 --> 00:03:58,382
That's what you live on.

123
00:03:58,562 --> 00:04:00,899
And Jake? Jake saves whatever is left

124
00:04:00,799 --> 00:04:02,350
at the end of the month.

125
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:04,956
Which is... nothing. Every single month.

126
00:04:05,036 --> 00:04:07,268
Because there's never anything left at the

127
00:04:07,168 --> 00:04:08,131
end of the month.

128
00:04:08,211 --> 00:04:10,264
And he wonders why it never works.

129
00:04:10,444 --> 00:04:12,675
The first transfer stings a little.

130
00:04:12,575 --> 00:04:14,562
Fifty dollars feels like a lot.

131
00:04:14,642 --> 00:04:15,934
But by the third one?

132
00:04:15,834 --> 00:04:17,353
You don't even notice it.

133
00:04:17,433 --> 00:04:19,672
Your spending just quietly adjusts to the

134
00:04:19,572 --> 00:04:20,403
money you see.

135
00:04:20,583 --> 00:04:22,844
Six paydays. Fifty dollars each.

136
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:24,565
That's three hundred dollars.

137
00:04:24,465 --> 00:04:26,105
And you didn't lift a finger.

138
00:04:26,185 --> 00:04:29,097
That's thirty percent of your goal. On autopilot.

139
00:04:29,177 --> 00:04:31,885
Every two weeks, without even thinking about it.

140
00:04:32,065 --> 00:04:34,555
Move number three. One Sunday evening,

141
00:04:34,455 --> 00:04:36,441
you open your bank statements.

142
00:04:36,521 --> 00:04:37,880
Three months of them.

143
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:38,960
With a cup of tea.

144
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:41,261
And you highlight every single thing that

145
00:04:41,161 --> 00:04:42,605
charges you automatically.

146
00:04:42,685 --> 00:04:45,539
Boring? Maybe. But this is where money hides.

147
00:04:45,719 --> 00:04:48,378
A streaming service you haven't opened since summer.

148
00:04:48,458 --> 00:04:51,502
A fitness app from your January motivation phase.

149
00:04:51,582 --> 00:04:54,295
A cloud storage upgrade you don't even

150
00:04:54,195 --> 00:04:55,945
need. Wait... seriously?

151
00:04:56,125 --> 00:04:57,587
Thirty dollars a month.

152
00:04:57,487 --> 00:04:58,830
Gone. Just like that.

153
00:04:58,910 --> 00:05:01,048
You didn't miss a single one of them.

154
00:05:01,128 --> 00:05:03,743
Over three months, that's ninety dollars back

155
00:05:03,643 --> 00:05:04,582
in your pocket.

156
00:05:04,662 --> 00:05:07,247
Thirty dollars a month doesn't sound like

157
00:05:07,147 --> 00:05:09,308
much... until you see it in a jar.

158
00:05:09,488 --> 00:05:12,593
Jake has the same subscriptions. Actually more.

159
00:05:12,673 --> 00:05:14,206
He knows he should check.

160
00:05:14,106 --> 00:05:16,556
He keeps saying he'll do it next weekend.

161
00:05:16,636 --> 00:05:18,391
Next weekend never comes.

162
00:05:18,471 --> 00:05:20,814
Meanwhile, the charges keep coming.

163
00:05:20,714 --> 00:05:21,968
Month after month.

164
00:05:22,148 --> 00:05:24,280
Move number four was actually fun.

165
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:27,580
You walked around your apartment and asked one question.

166
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:29,236
What here have I not used in

167
00:05:29,136 --> 00:05:29,921
a whole year?

168
00:05:30,101 --> 00:05:31,759
An old phone in a drawer.

169
00:05:31,839 --> 00:05:33,341
A bike you rode twice.

170
00:05:33,421 --> 00:05:35,805
A gaming console collecting dust.

171
00:05:35,705 --> 00:05:38,711
And a jacket with the tags still on. Ouch.

172
00:05:38,791 --> 00:05:41,437
Be honest... you've got a drawer like this too.

173
00:05:41,617 --> 00:05:43,545
You took photos in good light.

174
00:05:43,445 --> 00:05:45,373
You wrote honest descriptions.

175
00:05:45,453 --> 00:05:48,002
You priced them a little below everyone else.

176
00:05:48,082 --> 00:05:50,511
And you listed them all on a Saturday morning.

177
00:05:50,591 --> 00:05:52,000
Stuff you don't need...

178
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,252
but someone else does.

179
00:05:53,432 --> 00:05:55,503
By Sunday night, three of them were

180
00:05:55,403 --> 00:05:57,530
gone. The fourth sold that Thursday.

181
00:05:57,610 --> 00:05:59,546
Two hundred thirty-five dollars.

182
00:05:59,626 --> 00:06:01,016
Money you already had...

183
00:06:00,916 --> 00:06:02,735
it was just sitting in a drawer.

184
00:06:02,815 --> 00:06:04,643
Not bad for one weekend, right?

185
00:06:04,823 --> 00:06:06,565
And here's a little rule you made.

186
00:06:06,645 --> 00:06:09,225
Every dollar from selling stuff goes straight

187
00:06:09,125 --> 00:06:09,997
into the fund.

188
00:06:10,077 --> 00:06:12,068
Not 'just this once' into a dinner

189
00:06:11,968 --> 00:06:13,014
out. Straight in.

190
00:06:13,094 --> 00:06:16,354
Because money that feels like a bonus disappears fast.

191
00:06:16,534 --> 00:06:18,626
Move number five is where most of

192
00:06:18,526 --> 00:06:19,591
the money hides.

193
00:06:19,671 --> 00:06:20,623
Groceries.

194
00:06:20,703 --> 00:06:22,796
Not because you buy fancy food...

195
00:06:22,696 --> 00:06:25,152
but because you shopped without a plan.

196
00:06:25,232 --> 00:06:26,258
Let me show you.

197
00:06:26,438 --> 00:06:29,507
Before, you'd walk into the store hungry after work.

198
00:06:29,587 --> 00:06:31,220
No list. Just vibes.

199
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:32,793
Snacks, a fancy sauce,

200
00:06:32,693 --> 00:06:34,312
a second bag of chips...

201
00:06:34,212 --> 00:06:36,718
and half of it went bad in the fridge.

202
00:06:36,898 --> 00:06:38,862
Now? Every Sunday, ten minutes.

203
00:06:38,942 --> 00:06:40,856
You check what's already in the fridge.

204
00:06:40,936 --> 00:06:42,368
You plan five dinners.

205
00:06:42,268 --> 00:06:43,397
You write a list.

206
00:06:43,297 --> 00:06:44,972
And you never shop hungry.

207
00:06:45,152 --> 00:06:46,619
You cook a little extra,

208
00:06:46,519 --> 00:06:48,612
so dinner becomes tomorrow's lunch.

209
00:06:48,692 --> 00:06:51,261
You buy the store brand for basics.

210
00:06:51,161 --> 00:06:53,024
Rice, pasta, oats, beans.

211
00:06:53,104 --> 00:06:56,205
Same food. Just... without the expensive label.

212
00:06:56,285 --> 00:06:58,370
And nothing goes in the trash anymore.

213
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:00,994
The result? Your grocery bill drops by

214
00:07:00,894 --> 00:07:03,029
about twenty-five dollars a week.

215
00:07:03,109 --> 00:07:04,584
You're not eating worse.

216
00:07:04,484 --> 00:07:05,958
You're just not wasting.

217
00:07:06,038 --> 00:07:08,597
Twelve weeks... that's three hundred dollars.

218
00:07:08,777 --> 00:07:11,968
Meanwhile, Jake orders delivery four nights a week.

219
00:07:12,048 --> 00:07:14,390
Every order comes with a delivery fee,

220
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:15,866
a service fee, and a tip.

221
00:07:15,946 --> 00:07:19,247
He's always surprised the burger cost twenty-eight dollars.

222
00:07:19,327 --> 00:07:21,974
That's over four hundred dollars a month.

223
00:07:21,874 --> 00:07:23,030
Just on delivery.

224
00:07:23,210 --> 00:07:25,846
Move number six. The no-spend weekend.

225
00:07:25,926 --> 00:07:27,906
Once a month, from Friday night to

226
00:07:27,806 --> 00:07:29,785
Sunday night... you spend nothing.

227
00:07:29,865 --> 00:07:31,517
No shopping. No takeout.

228
00:07:31,417 --> 00:07:33,393
No 'quick little' coffee run.

229
00:07:33,473 --> 00:07:34,579
It's a little game.

230
00:07:34,479 --> 00:07:36,432
And you're surprisingly good at it.

231
00:07:36,612 --> 00:07:38,286
Sounds boring? It wasn't.

232
00:07:38,366 --> 00:07:40,613
You went hiking. You had friends over

233
00:07:40,513 --> 00:07:42,935
for a movie night with homemade popcorn.

234
00:07:43,015 --> 00:07:45,074
You finally read that book that's been

235
00:07:44,974 --> 00:07:46,517
on your shelf for two years.

236
00:07:46,697 --> 00:07:48,882
A normal weekend used to cost you

237
00:07:48,782 --> 00:07:50,903
around twenty-five dollars more.

238
00:07:50,983 --> 00:07:54,343
Three no-spend weekends? Seventy-five dollars.

239
00:07:54,423 --> 00:07:56,743
And honestly... some of the best weekends

240
00:07:56,643 --> 00:07:57,825
you've had all year.

241
00:07:58,005 --> 00:07:59,037
Okay. Ready?

242
00:07:59,117 --> 00:08:00,471
Let's add it all up.

243
00:08:00,551 --> 00:08:03,256
Fifty dollars every payday. Three hundred.

244
00:08:03,336 --> 00:08:05,288
Groceries. Another three hundred.

245
00:08:05,468 --> 00:08:08,020
Stuff you sold. Two hundred thirty-five.

246
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:10,450
Subscriptions you cancelled. Ninety.

247
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:13,013
No-spend weekends. Seventy-five.

248
00:08:13,093 --> 00:08:14,085
Wait for it...

249
00:08:14,265 --> 00:08:14,979
Total?

250
00:08:15,059 --> 00:08:17,043
Exactly one thousand dollars.

251
00:08:17,123 --> 00:08:19,544
In ninety days. On the same paycheck

252
00:08:19,444 --> 00:08:20,511
you always had.

253
00:08:20,591 --> 00:08:23,237
Same job. Same city. Same paycheck.

254
00:08:23,417 --> 00:08:24,756
You didn't get a raise.

255
00:08:24,836 --> 00:08:26,657
You didn't stop seeing your friends.

256
00:08:26,737 --> 00:08:28,877
You didn't eat rice and beans every

257
00:08:28,777 --> 00:08:30,918
day. You just... stopped the leaks.

258
00:08:31,098 --> 00:08:32,842
And then, two weeks later...

259
00:08:32,742 --> 00:08:34,016
that grinding sound.

260
00:08:34,096 --> 00:08:35,531
Six hundred dollars.

261
00:08:35,611 --> 00:08:37,169
You pay it. You drive home.

262
00:08:37,069 --> 00:08:38,033
You make dinner.

263
00:08:38,113 --> 00:08:41,078
No credit card. No panic. No interest.

264
00:08:41,258 --> 00:08:43,167
And here's the most important part.

265
00:08:43,247 --> 00:08:45,803
You don't stop. Your fifty dollars keeps

266
00:08:45,703 --> 00:08:47,215
going in, every payday.

267
00:08:47,295 --> 00:08:49,280
Because now the goal is to refill

268
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:50,936
the fund... and then grow it.

269
00:08:51,116 --> 00:08:53,929
Next stop? One full month of expenses.

270
00:08:54,009 --> 00:08:56,012
Then three months. Then six.

271
00:08:56,092 --> 00:08:58,053
Same moves. Just... more time.

272
00:08:58,233 --> 00:09:00,098
And Jake? Three months from now,

273
00:08:59,998 --> 00:09:01,974
he's still paying for that repair.

274
00:09:02,054 --> 00:09:03,719
Plus interest. Plus stress.

275
00:09:03,799 --> 00:09:06,185
He tells himself he'll start saving after

276
00:09:06,085 --> 00:09:06,966
it's paid off.

277
00:09:07,046 --> 00:09:08,618
And every month he waits,

278
00:09:08,518 --> 00:09:10,384
the next surprise gets closer.

279
00:09:10,564 --> 00:09:11,837
But you know the truth.

280
00:09:11,917 --> 00:09:14,080
There's never a perfect moment to start.

281
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:16,551
There's only today. With fifty dollars.

282
00:09:16,451 --> 00:09:17,433
Or even twenty.

283
00:09:17,613 --> 00:09:19,124
So here's your homework.

284
00:09:19,024 --> 00:09:20,358
Tonight. Ten minutes.

285
00:09:20,438 --> 00:09:22,853
Open a separate savings account and give

286
00:09:22,753 --> 00:09:23,432
it a name.

287
00:09:23,512 --> 00:09:26,038
Set up one automatic transfer for your

288
00:09:25,938 --> 00:09:27,571
next payday. Any amount.

289
00:09:27,751 --> 00:09:30,339
Then do one more move this week. Just one.

290
00:09:30,419 --> 00:09:33,184
Cancel a subscription. Or sell one thing.

291
00:09:33,084 --> 00:09:34,874
Or write one grocery list.

292
00:09:34,954 --> 00:09:36,696
Small moves. Ninety days.

293
00:09:36,596 --> 00:09:38,074
One thousand dollars.

294
00:09:38,254 --> 00:09:40,650
If this helped, subscribe for one simple

295
00:09:40,550 --> 00:09:41,855
money move every day.

296
00:09:41,935 --> 00:09:43,659
And tell me in the comments...

297
00:09:43,559 --> 00:09:46,529
what's the surprise expense that hit you the hardest?

298
00:09:46,609 --> 00:09:48,095
I read every comment.
